    "title": "Missing 84-year-old Alcoa woman found dead - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Gregory Raucoules ,\n5 hours ago\n(UPDATE) — The Tennessee Bureau of Investigation announced that 84-year-old Jacqueline Wiggins was found dead. She had been the subject of a Silver Alert after she was reported missing from her Alcoa home on Wednesday.\nALCOA, Tenn. (WATE) — An 84-year-old Alcoa woman was reported missing on Wednesday and authorities are asking for the public’s help to locate her.\nThe Tennessee Bureau of Investigation has issued a Silver Alert for Jacqueline Wiggins.\nShe was last seen Wednesday afternoon on E. Newcomen Street in Alcoa and is believed to be on foot.\nWiggins was last seen wearing a black shirt and pink pajama pants with red and yellow socks but no shoes. She is described as standing 5’1″ tall with brown eyes and gray hair.\nCredit: Alcoa Police Department She has a medical condition that may impair her ability to get home safely without assistance.\nThe Alcoa Police Department asked residents in the Hall and Springbrook communities to check home security footage as well as in their basements, under decks, backyards and cars for any sign of Wiggins.\n▶ See more top stories on WATE.com Anyone who may have seen her is asked to call the Alcoa Police Department at 865-738-5724 or TBI at 1-800-TBI-FIND.\nAuthorities say nearly 400 people go missing in Tennessee each year. Check this list of people missing from East Tennessee to see if you can help locate someone.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WATE 6 On Your Side.\n2",
    "summary": "(UPDATE) — The Tennessee Bureau of Investigation announced that 84-year-old Jacqueline Wiggins was found dead. Wiggins was last seen wearing a black shirt and pink pajama pants with red and yellow socks but no shoes. Credit: Alcoa Police Department She has a medical condition that may impair her ability to get home safely without assistance. The Alcoa Police Department asked residents in the Hall and Springbrook communities to check home security footage as well as in their basements, under decks, backyards and cars for any sign of Wiggins.",
